    "font-title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 740, "top": 660,
      "text": "GLYPH COVERAGE — verified",
      "fontColor": "#c8d8f0", "fontSize": 13
    },
    "font-body": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 740, "top": 686,
      "text": "All four pupil glyphs are inside the ranges\nJetBrains Mono actually ships:\n\n  ◧  U+25E7   in 25e6–25eb\n  ◨  U+25E8   in 25e6–25eb\n  ●  U+25CF   in 25ce–25cf\n  ◎  U+25CE   in 25ce–25cf\n\nSame family as the rain glyphs, so no new font\nasset and no licenses.yaml entry. Monospace\nadvance width holds, so the eyes do not jitter\nhorizontally as they change.",
      "fontColor": "#c8d0e0", "fontSize": 11
    },

    "why-title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 40, "top": 850,
      "text": "WHY THIS EARNS ITS KEEP",
      "fontColor": "#c8d8f0", "fontSize": 13
    },
    "why-body": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 40, "top": 876,
      "text": "It is not decoration — it is a second, cheaper channel for the same signal. Rain says HOW HARD the session is working;\ngaze says WHAT IT IS WORKING ON. And it makes the digest boundary visible: Clide looks at the conversation because that is\nall he is given. When you ask him about a tool call and he cannot answer, the fact that he was never looking at it is\nalready on screen — the limitation reads as character rather than as a bug.\n\nCost: one enum on top of Epic A's state table (gazeLeft / forward / gazeRight / none) and a 4-frame pupil cycle. No new\nstate machine — it rides the existing busy + partial- signals Epic B already binds.",
      "fontColor": "#c8d0e0", "fontSize": 11
    },

    "lean-title": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 740, "top": 850,
      "text": "THE LEAN — a happy accident, kept",
      "fontColor": "#c8d8f0", "fontSize": 13
    },
    "lean-body": {
      "type": "Text",
      "left": 740, "top": 876,
      "text": "The tilt in the first wireframe was a coordinate\nslip — the mouth landed off the eye axis. It read\nas a head-tilt, so it stays, now deliberate.\n\nOne number: mouth x-offset from the eye centre.\n  lean left   −8px   reading the conversation\n  upright      0px   addressed, or idle\n  lean right  +8px   glancing at the detail view\n\nAnimate the offset rather than snapping it and\nthe lean becomes the transition itself. It costs\nnothing — the mouth is already positioned per\nframe — and it makes attention legible at a size\nwhere pupils alone are only a few pixels.",
      "fontColor": "#c8d0e0", "fontSize": 11
    }
  }
